{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"undamaging-dissimilar-sidewinder","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Why\n\n`[operator-attested]` (ADR-0043) is the acceptance-criteria qualifier that tells the completion\nverifier \"this criterion's done-evidence inherently lives OUTSIDE the codebase, so accept a\nrecorded attestation instead of hunting for code proof\".\n\nThe word `operator` mis-signals. Across this repo it means a *human specifically* — \"the\noperator runbook\", \"the operator guide\", \"the operator escape hatch\", \"the operator can\" — and\nthe detector even carries a marker family named `human`. Agents therefore read\n`[operator-attested]` as \"a person must do this\", when in fact an agent recording a Gerrit vote\noutcome or a live-run result attests exactly as well. The tag names the wrong axis: WHO attests,\nrather than WHERE the evidence lives.\n\nRename the canonical tag to `[non-codebase]`, the missing half of a pair the vocabulary already\nhas. `[codebase]` is ALREADY a recognized tag (ADR-0043 §exact-match; `completion_verifier.md:95`;\n`plan_review_evidence_kind.md:56`; used on seven ACs of ticket 7226 in the corpus). It is\nprompt-level only — no regex parses it — but it is established author vocabulary, so\n`[non-codebase]` is not a coinage.\n\nThe new name also RESISTS the attestation-laundering anti-pattern by construction. To tag a\ncriterion whose proof is `tests/unit/test_scan_scoping.py` (the live bug 2f56 case), an author\nmust assert that file is \"non-codebase\" — self-evidently false. The name does the\n`det_attestation_launder` guard's work in the author's head before the guard ever runs.\n\n`[operator-attested]` is RETAINED as an accepted but UNDOCUMENTED compatibility alias. This is\nnot optional: 827 of 4,285 tickets in the live store (19%) carry the tag. Those are immutable\nevent-sourced records; a hard cutover would silently reclassify every one of their attested ACs\nas codebase-verifiable and break their close path.\n\n## Scope decisions (operator-approved)\n\n- The completion-verdict `kind` wire value `\"operator-attested\"` -> `\"non-codebase\"` IS in scope.\n  Blast radius is ONE comparison site (`src/rebar/audit/page.py:329`); the schema declares `kind`\n  as unconstrained `\"type\": \"string\"` with no enum. Sequenced reader-widen BEFORE emitter-change\n  so legacy persisted verdicts keep rendering.\n- Python identifiers are NOT renamed: the `det_operator_attested` module,\n  `_OPERATOR_ATTESTED_TAG_RE`, `_OPERATOR_ATTESTED_AC_RE`, the `operator_attested` finding\n  attribute, the `operator_attested_gaps` coverage key. This keeps the object-identity re-export\n  seam and its tests untouched, and removes 5 files from the change set entirely\n  (`det_citation.py`, `det_clarity.py`, `test_det_citation.py`, `test_decide_ops_seam.py`,\n  `test_plan_review_portability.py` reference only the symbols, never the tag string).\n\n## What makes this tractable\n\nAll four deterministic consumers share the IDENTICAL compiled regex object — verified: `decide_ops`,\n`workflow_ops`, `det_attestation_launder`, and `det_measurement_provenance` each satisfy `is`\nagainst `det_operator_attested._OPERATOR_ATTESTED_TAG_RE`. Widening that ONE pattern propagates\neverywhere with no other code change, and needs no new branch anywhere — which is why\n`det_floor.py` (793 of its 800-line hard cap) and `decide_ops.plan_review_decide` (complexity 21,\ngrandfathered at threshold 15) require no edits and neither gate fires.\n\n## Inventory\n\n85 tracked files carry the token (ticket store excluded), confirmed exhaustive by an independent\nblind sweep that returned ZERO hits for camelCase, space-separated, all seven non-ASCII hyphen\nvariants, and line-wrapped / implicitly-concatenated splits. Roughly 20 files need edits; the rest\nare historical or pass-through-compatible.\n\n- HISTORICAL, no change: ADRs 0043/0046/0049/0052/0059/0061/0098 and the generated ADR index;\n  `CHANGELOG.md`; all of `docs/experiments/plan-review-gate/` (harness, `runs/*.jsonl`,\n  `runs/*.json` — frozen corpora whose filenames and `operator-attested-retag` labels are\n  load-bearing); `docs/research/`; the captured-sample fixtures in\n  `tests/fixtures/{cloud_adf,dc_wiki}_corpus/` (`scripts/check_criteria_vocabulary.py:47-52`\n  states rewriting them falsifies the fixture).\n- COMPATIBILITY, accept both: the canonical regex; `.rebar/criteria_routing.json:75` trigger;\n  `.rebar/prompts/plan-review-project-measurement-provenance.md`; recognition-side prompts\n  (`completion_verifier.md`, `plan_review_evidence_kind.md`, `plan_review_ac_process_gate.md`);\n  the eval-spec fixtures; `audit/page.py`; ~20 detector/close-guard tests that feed the old tag\n  as input and assert on behavior.\n- CUTOVER: `writing-a-passing-plan.md`; emit-side prompts E2/E6/F1; `coach_moves.py` move 14;\n  `txn.py` + `completion_reconcile.py` message strings; the generated criteria guide (via its\n  generator); the ~10 tests pinning those literal strings.\n\n`operator-attested-retag` is a DIFFERENT token family (an edit-classification label in the\nexperiment harnesses) and must NOT be renamed.\n\n## Implementation traps (each costs a review cycle if missed)\n\n1. The widened alternation MUST be non-capturing: `\\[(?:non-codebase|operator-attested)\\]`. A\n   capturing group turns `decide_ops.operator_attested_ac_texts`'s `findall()` into a list of\n   TUPLES and breaks enrichment. Verified empirically.\n2. `tests/unit/test_measurement_provenance_criterion.py` pins SHA256 digests AND exact line counts\n   for contract text in five files. Its `_contract_lines()` selector keeps lines containing\n   `\"OPERATOR-ATTESTED RULE\"` or `\"[operator-attested]\"` — cut the prompts over without widening\n   the selector and it matches ZERO lines, failing on the count assertion with a misleading\n   \"expected 1 contract line(s), found 0\".\n3. `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` is GENERATED\n   (`python -m rebar.llm.plan_review.registry regenerate-criteria-guide`) from the reviewer prompt\n   `.md` files, and is gated three ways: `registry.validate_criteria_guide(...) == []` asserted in\n   five test files, content pins in `tests/unit/test_generated_surface_markers.py`, and the digest\n   pin above. Prompt edit + regeneration + pin updates must land in ONE commit.\n4. `.rebar/criteria_routing.json:75` is an activation TRIGGER\n   (`text_all: [\"\\\\[operator-attested\\\\]\"]`). Not widened => `[non-codebase]` plans SILENTLY skip\n   `project.measurement-provenance` rather than erroring.\n5. `src/rebar/_guides/writing-a-passing-plan.md:204` quotes the `coach_moves.py` move-14 template\n   VERBATIM. Both must change identically or the guide misquotes real coach output.\n6. The `[operator_attested]` underscore form is a DELIBERATE non-match asserted in ~15 places\n   (ADR-0043 §46). `[non-codebase]` needs its own near-miss policy decided and tested.\n7. Four further CI gates fire on files this touches and contain none of the token:\n   `validate-routing`, the `reviewers/index.json` drift gate, `_eval-discipline.yml`, and\n   `prompt-eval.yml`. `src/rebar/_guides/criterion-pins.json` is a digest manifest with zero\n   textual hits (it does not currently pin `evidence-kind` or `project.measurement-provenance`,\n   so those two criteria alone will not break it).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The canonical tag matcher accepts `[non-codebase]` and `[operator-attested]`\n      case-insensitively, rejects the `[operator_attested]` near-miss, and uses a NON-capturing\n      alternation so `findall()` arity is unchanged — proved by a unit test asserting\n      `operator_attested_ac_texts` returns a list of `str`, not tuples.\n- [ ] Every existing test that feeds `[operator-attested]` passes UNCHANGED, demonstrating the\n      alias still works.\n- [ ] `[non-codebase]` and `[operator-attested]` produce identical outcomes across the plan-time\n      lint, the laundering guard, the provenance guard, the AC-checkbox precheck, and the close\n      path — proved by parametrizing those suites over both spellings.\n- [ ] `project.measurement-provenance` fires on a `[non-codebase]`-tagged plan (the\n      `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` trigger was widened), proved by a test asserting the trigger\n      matches both spellings.\n- [ ] Author-facing guidance emits `[non-codebase]` only; `[operator-attested]` appears in NO\n      documented surface, enforced by a NEGATIVE test over the packaged guide.\n- [ ] `src/rebar/audit/page.py` renders a legacy `kind: \"operator-attested\"` verdict identically\n      to a new `kind: \"non-codebase\"` one, proved by a test over both fixture shapes.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass, and the generated criteria guide is regenerated with\n      its parity check and contract digests updated in the same commit.","id":"6f5a-4a49-7bbf-4a30","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Rename the [operator-attested] AC qualifier to [non-codebase]"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787185860046005001,"uuid":"85023b37-7d0b-4195-9d45-af5191c5e38d"}